Q: Can I temporarily override DNS resolution within a .NET application? A: <p>This question is almost 15 years old, and the good news is that now there is a way!</p>
<p>The solution I tried comes from <a href="https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/discussions/98425#discussion-6217857" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/discussions/98425#discussion-6217857</a> . I'm copying it here in case that link dies some day:</p>
<pre><code>public async Task&lt;HttpResponseMessage&gt; GetFromIp(IPAddress ip, Uri uri, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    var client = new HttpClient(SocketHandlerWithIpPreference(ip));
    var request = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, uri);
    return await client.SendAsync(request, cancellationToken);
}

private SocketsHttpHandler SocketHandlerWithIpPreference(IPAddress ip) =&gt; new()
{
    ConnectCallback = async (context, cancellationToken) =&gt;
    {
        // Adapt that part (the 7 lines below) to your need: perhaps you don't need this DNS resolution at all (if you want to always override)
        var dnsEntries = await Dns.GetHostEntryAsync(context.DnsEndPoint.Host, AddressFamily.Unspecified, cancellationToken);

        var addresses = dnsEntries.AddressList;
        if (addresses.Contains(ip))
        {
            addresses = [ip];
        }

        var socket = new Socket(SocketType.Stream, ProtocolType.Tcp);

        try
        {
            await socket.ConnectAsync(addresses, context.DnsEndPoint.Port, cancellationToken);
            return new NetworkStream(socket, ownsSocket: true);
        }
        catch
        {
            socket.Dispose();
            throw;
        }
    }
};
</code></pre>

Q: How to create a custom input method Engine (IME) in IBUS for ubuntu / kali_2025 (debian) A: <p>Is there a documentation? Not directly. <a href="https://ibus.github.io/docs/ibus-1.5/index.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Here gobject-introspectable C apis</a> are available. If you want to use any high-level language like python, then you need to enable gobject-introspection capability for that language. I am using Kali 2022 with Debian 12 bookworm. As Ubuntu is also Debian based, the following process should work on both systems.</p>
<p>Lets assume you prefer to create a custom input method for ibus in python. Then you can go to <a href="https://lazka.github.io/pgi-docs/#IBus-1.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer">PyGObject api for gtk-3</a> and find necessary classes and methods exposed by GObject introspection via Python.  If you want to use javascript then you can use <a href="https://gjs.guide/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GNOME JavaScript runtime</a> for creating an ibus input method engine.</p>
<h1>Necessary Packages to install :</h1>
<p>First you have make sure <code>dbus</code> is installed in your system. It is a package which manages inter-process-communication in linux as <code>ibus</code> will interact with your application via <code>dbus</code> .</p>
<p>If you choose to use Python, then you have to install following packages :</p>
<p><code>sudo apt install ibus python3-gi python3-gi-cairo gir1.2-ibus-1.0</code>.</p>
<p>-- <code>ibus</code> will install intelligent input bus (ibus) daemon into your system.</p>
<p>-- <code>python3-gi</code> .This is a linux pacakge to interact with GObject based softwares.It will bring system-wide gobject introspection capability to python.</p>
<p>-- <code>gir1.2-ibus-1.0</code> will make ibus gobject introspection data available to higher level languages like python,rust,gjs-javascript-runtime etc.</p>
<p><em><strong>For IBus engine development in a Conda environment :</strong></em></p>
<p>If you are running ibus-daemon and developing the ibus-custom-engine inside a conda environment then you have to install <code>PyGObjet</code> via pip3 in that specific conda environment also alongside <code>python3-gi</code> system-wide pacakge.<code>PyGObject</code> is <code>pip</code> python package, not a linux system package.You need <code>PyGObject</code> because Python installation inside a conda environment won't have access to python3-gi system-wide package. So  <code>import gi</code> will fail inside a conda environment.</p>
<h1>Sample Engine Code :</h1>
<p>you can use the following input method engine code as a base. Make this .py file <code>executable</code> and put <code>#!/usr/bin/env python3</code> shebang at the top of the file</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>
#!/usr/bin/env python3

import gi

gi.require_version(&quot;IBus&quot;, &quot;1.0&quot;)
gi.require_version(&quot;GioUnix&quot;,&quot;2.0&quot;)

from gi.repository import IBus, GObject, GLib

class SampleEngine(IBus.Engine):

     def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()


    def do_focus_in(self):
        #callback hook fires when you focus an input
        pass


    def do_enable(self):
        # Fires when ibus enable the engine(when you select this input method)
        pass

    def do_process_key_event(self,keyval:int,keyCode:int,state:int) -&gt; bool : 
       #fires when a key event occurs.
       pass

    def main():
        IBus.init()
        bus = IBus.Bus.new()
        factory = IBus.Factory.new(bus.get_connection())
        factory.add_engine(&quot;ibus_engine_custom&quot;, SampleEngine) 

        # org.freedesktop.IBus.MyEngine is the requested name 
        # this name must match with the one given in the component-XML file
        bus.request_name(&quot;org.freedesktop.IBus.MySampleEngine&quot;, 0)   
        main_loop = GLib.MainLoop()
        main_loop.run()


if __name__ == &quot;__main__&quot;:
    main()
</code></pre>
<p>Then you have to create a component xml file and put in in <code>/usr/share/ibus/components</code> directory so that ibus can find your engine when <code>ibus-daemon</code> starts and make your engine available for you to select in ibus-setup user interface.</p>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?xml version=&quot;1.0&quot; encoding=&quot;utf-8&quot;?&gt;
&lt;component&gt;
  &lt;name&gt;org.freedesktop.IBus.MySampleEngine&lt;/name&gt;
  &lt;exec&gt;/usr/share/ibus-sample/path_to_your_engine_file.py&lt;/exec&gt;
  &lt;description&gt;An IBus engine example&lt;/description&gt;
  &lt;license&gt;GPL&lt;/license&gt;
  &lt;author&gt;Al-zami rahman &amp;lt;alzami.shimanta@gmail.com&amp;gt;&lt;/author&gt;
  &lt;homepage&gt;https://your-engine-site.com&lt;/homepage&gt;
  &lt;version&gt;1.0&lt;/version&gt;
  &lt;engines&gt;
    &lt;engine&gt;
      &lt;name&gt;ibus_engine_custom&lt;/name&gt;
      &lt;language&gt;bn&lt;/language&gt;
      &lt;longname&gt;ibus-custom-engine&lt;/longname&gt;
      &lt;author&gt;Al-zami rahman&lt;/author&gt;
      &lt;layout&gt;us&lt;/layout&gt;
      &lt;description&gt;Write letters with it&lt;/description&gt;
      &lt;rank&gt;100&lt;/rank&gt;
    &lt;/engine&gt;
  &lt;/engines&gt;
&lt;/component&gt;


</code></pre>

Q: Is there a way to turn a CSV TAQ file back into a SAS native file with the same properities? A: <p>It sounds like you're basically just trying to import a CSV file and <code>PROC IMPORT</code> doesn't guess the columns correctly. One option to try first is to increase the number of guessing rows. This will check more data to guess its type. For example:</p>
<pre><code>proc import
    file='file.csv'
    out=imported_file
    dbms=csv
    replace;
    guessingrows=1000;
run;
</code></pre>
<p>If it still doesn't get the values right, then you'll need to manually import the data to define each column, name, and type. <a href="https://wrds-www.wharton.upenn.edu/pages/about/data-vendors/nyse-trade-and-quote-taq/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">TAQ files all come with a data dictionary</a> so you can use that to identify the column, type, and format. For example, suppose your CSV looked like this:</p>
<pre><code>symbol,timestamp,price
AAPL,2026-01-02:9:30:00,260.20
AAPL,2026-01-02:9:30:15,260.59
AAPL,2026-01-02:9:30:30,261.29
</code></pre>
<p>You can import it as a SAS file with a DATA step:</p>
<pre><code>data imported_file;
    length symbol $4 timestamp price 8;
    format timestamp datetime.;

    infile 'file.csv' dlm=',' dsd firstobs=2;

    input symbol$ timestamp:anydtdtm. price;
run;
</code></pre>
<p>Note that for the format we choose <code>anydtdtm.</code> to read the timestamp, which is one of the <a href="https://go.documentation.sas.com/doc/da/pgmsascdc/v_071/leforinforref/p13eggx7qc7l6in1oprinvadzubl.htm" rel="nofollow noreferrer">many datetime formats SAS can read </a>and is usually a very good one to start with.</p>
<p>I don't have the ability to download a TAQ file since I am not a student so this really is just a simple exercise in reading a csv file using a DATA step.</p>

Q: How to apply color on text in Markdown A: <p>I like the idea of redefining existing tags if they're unused due to the fact that the text is cleaner, at the expense of existing tags.  The inline styling works but creates a lot of noise when reading the raw text.</p>
<p>Using VSCode I've found that custom single-letter tags, supported by a small <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> section at the top, works well with a minimum of noise, especially for spot colour, e.g.</p>
<pre class="lang-markdown prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;style&gt;
r { color: Red }
o { color: Orange }
g { color: Green }
&lt;/style&gt;

# TODOs:

- &lt;r&gt;TODO:&lt;/r&gt; Important thing to do
- &lt;o&gt;TODO:&lt;/o&gt; Less important thing to do
- &lt;g&gt;DONE:&lt;/g&gt; Breath deeply and improve karma
</code></pre>
<p>In cases where this clashes with other styling (and as mentioned in another answer) you can remove the default styling first and then add the colour back in. e.g. <code>&lt;s&gt;</code> will add strikethrough by default. The following removes this and adds back the colour:</p>
<pre><code>s { text-decoration: none; color: Teal; }
</code></pre>
<p>My use-case is orgmode-ish in-app note taking during development but I guess it might work elsewhere?</p>

Q: Using a repeated block of code as function in xsl A: <p>In XSLT 1.0 the closest thing to a function is a named template, which you can declare like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;xsl:template name=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
  &lt;xsl:param name=&quot;w&quot;&gt;
  &lt;xsl:if test=&quot;$w &gt; 10&quot;&gt;Greater than ten&lt;/xsl:if&gt;
&lt;/xsl:template&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and you can invoke it like this:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;xsl:call-template name=&quot;content&quot;&gt;
   &lt;xsl:with-param name=&quot;w&quot; select=&quot;20&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/xsl:call-template&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>From XSLT 2.0 onwards you can also declare a named function: this uses <code>xsl:function</code> in place of <code>xsl:template</code>, and is invoked using an XPath function call.</p>
